<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/7th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Battle of Dongola</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/652">652</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Siege of Old Dongola, Siege of Dongola (652)</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Battle of Dongola was the siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-dongola">Old Dongola</a>, capital of the Nubian kingdom of Makuria, by the Arab army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdallah-ibn-sad-ibn-abi-sarh">Abdallah ibn Sa'd ibn Abi Sarh</a>, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, in 652. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> bombarded the city with a catapult and damaged its church, but Nubian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archery">archers</a> inflicted heavy losses and the city held. The siege ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baqt">Baqt treaty</a>, which left Makuria independent for six centuries.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Relations between Makuria and Rashidun Egypt had begun badly with the first battle of Dongola in 642. After their defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, and something of a peace was in place by 645. According to the 14th-century Arab-Egyptian historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">al-Maqrizi</a>, Makuria then broke the truce, and Abdallah ibn Sa'd, successor of the first governor of Arab Egypt, invaded to bring the Makurians to heel. Northern and central Nubia were at that time united under the Makurian king Qalidurut.</p><p>Archaeology shows that Dongola was well fortified in the seventh century, with walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mudbrick</a> faced with stone at least 6 metres high and 4 metres wide at the base, and round corner towers projecting 8 metres from the wall. The towers may have been added after the siege, perhaps in response to it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Abdallah marched on Dongola with 5,000 men, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heavy-cavalry">heavy cavalry</a> and a catapult, or manjaniq, probably a traction <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trebuchet">trebuchet</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">al-Maqrizi</a> says the Makurians had never seen before. His cavalry faced the task of storming a walled city held by the Nubian archers. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catapult">Catapult</a> fire damaged the town's cathedral; a damaged church of the mid-seventh century has been found outside the remains of the walls.</p><p>The siege ended in a pitched battle in which Abdallah's forces suffered heavy casualties, and Qalidurut did not sue for peace. The 9th-century Egyptian historian Ibn 'Abd al-Hakam wrote that Abdallah made terms because he was unable to defeat them, and the 10th-century Shiite historian Ahmad al-Kufi wrote that the Muslims had never before suffered such a loss.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Abdallah called off the siege in exchange for a negotiated pact, the baqt. It set up trade between Muslim Egypt and Christian Nubia, in which Egypt sent wheat, barley, wine, horses and linen and Nubia sent 360 slaves a year. The baqt had no precedent in the early history of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>, and Nubia's status as a land free from conquest was equally new; its rulers dealt with Muslim rulers on equal terms until the 12th century, when Nubian power began to wane.</p><p>In later centuries Muslim historians recast the siege as a victory, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">al-Maqrizi</a> describing Qalidurut coming out submissively to seek terms, a version that may stem from confusing the events of 652 with the conflicts between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> in the late 13th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second battle of Dongola” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
